- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Container index and iterator bounds checking
WHAT: Accessing containers or arrays using indices or iterators that are outside the valid range (for example, pos >= tableSize) causes out-of-bounds reads/writes.
WHY: Out-of-bounds access can corrupt memory, trigger undefined behavior, and lead to exploitable vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Always validate indices and iterator positions against container size before access; prefer APIs or templates that deduce array bounds at compile time and centralize bounds checks in a single, reused function.
EXAMPLE:
Before (unsafe): `table[pos] = value;`
After (safe): `if (pos >= tableSize) { /* handle error */ return; } table[pos] = value;`

- Prevent data races on adjacent C++ bit-fields in multithreaded code
WHAT: Concurrent writes to different C++ bit-fields in the same storage unit (for example, adjacent bit-fields in a struct) from multiple threads cause data races and undefined behavior.
WHY: Data races can lead to unpredictable behavior, corrupted state, and exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities that are hard to diagnose.
HOW: Do not access adjacent bit-fields from multiple threads without synchronization; either protect all accesses to the containing object with the same mutex, or separate bit-fields by inserting a non–bit-field member so each bit-field occupies its own storage unit.
EXAMPLE:
Before: struct S { unsigned f1:2; unsigned f2:2; }; // f1 and f2 may share storage and be written in different threads.
After: struct S { unsigned f1:2; unsigned pad; unsigned f2:2; }; // or guard all accesses to S with a single shared mutex.

- Prevent data races when accessing shared bit-fields from multiple threads
WHAT: Unsynchronized read-modify-write operations on shared bit-fields from multiple threads can interleave, causing lost updates and inconsistent flag values.
WHY: Data races lead to undefined behavior, corrupted state, and security issues such as incorrect authorization flags or bypassed checks.
HOW: Treat bit-fields as shared mutable state; protect all reads and writes with the same synchronization primitive (e.g., a mutex) or use atomic types/operations that cover the entire flags object.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `flags.s.flag1 = 1;` and `flags.s.flag2 = 2;` written from different threads without locking. After: `std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(flags.mutex); flags.s.flag1 = 1;` and similarly for `flag2` using the same mutex.

- Avoid information leakage when passing objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Failing to fully initialize all fields (including padding and reserved fields) of objects or structs before copying them across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized memory contents.
WHY: Attackers can read sensitive kernel or process memory, potentially exposing secrets, internal state, or enabling further exploitation.
HOW: Before returning or sending any struct/class instance to less-trusted code, explicitly initialize or zero all fields (including padding/reserved areas), or use APIs/types that guarantee zero-initialization.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `MyStruct s; some_fields_set(s); send_to_user(s);` After: `MyStruct s{}; some_fields_set(s); secure_memset_unused_fields(s); send_to_user(s);`

- Close files when they are no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to call std::basic_filebuf<T>::close() (or to let std::fstream / std::ifstream / std::ofstream destructors run) leaves files open when execution paths (such as std::terminate/std::abort) bypass normal destruction.
WHY: Unclosed files can leak resources, exhaust file descriptors, corrupt data, or leave sensitive data in an inconsistent or exposed state.
HOW: Ensure every successful std::basic_filebuf<T>::open() is matched with close(), or manage file streams by value so their destructors run; for abnormal termination paths (e.g., std::terminate), explicitly close files before terminating.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant): std::fstream f(name); /* use f */ std::terminate();
After (compliant): std::fstream f(name); /* use f */ f.close(); std::terminate();



